          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The theorem (
              %a{:href => "quantum+channel#QuantumChannelsAsPartialTracesOfUnitariesOnTensors"}> here
              ) that every endo-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channel"} quantum channel
              has an environmental representation is sometimes advertized with the addendum that “… and such that the environment may be chosen to be in a pure state”. But existing general proofs actually produce
              %em only
              such environmental representations. For environments in non-pure states it is not clear that they can environmentally represent all quantum channels, and for noisy/unistochastic channels it is not to be expected that they exhaust all quantum channels.
            %p
              But
              %a{:href => "#Müller-HermesPerry19"} Müller-Hermes &amp; Perry 2019
              show that at least all
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+quantum+channels"} unital quantum channels
              on single
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
              can be realized as noisy/unistochastic channels (with a bath of size at least 4).
          %p
          #DQC1Model.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (DQC1 model)
              %br/
              But the idea of exploring
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
              on (a few or just single) clean
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
              coupled to an environment in a maximally
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+state"} mixed state
              goes back already to
              %a{:href => "#KnillLaflamme98"} Knill &amp; Laflamme 1998
              (motivated by discussion of
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+resonance+qbits"} spin resonance qbits
              %em deterministic quantum computation with one quantum bit
              (abbreviated “
              %em> DQC1
              ”, now also used for the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+complexity+theory"} quantum complexity
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+class"} class
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Shor08"} Shor 2008
            %blockquote
              %p
                The DQC1 model is believed to be a very restricted, but still genuinely quantum, computing model – a sub-universal quantum computing model whose power is something between classical computation and universal quantum computation. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "#FKMNTT18"}> FKMNTT18, p. 2
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %p
              The communities using these different terminologies for closely related ideas may have been somewhat disconnected. A proposal to look at DQC1 in terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channels"} quantum channels
              seems to not appear not before
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "#XuerebCampbellGooldXuereb23"} Xuereb, Campbell, Goold &amp; Xuereb 2023
              %a{:href => "#FuHeLiLuo23"} Fu, He, Li &amp; Luo 2023
              (and the “unistochastic” terminology is not used there).
